        color = 0xFFB5543A,
        visual = VisualStyle.CONNECTIONS,
        summary = """
## The big idea

Dale Carnegie's central claim is simple and a little uncomfortable: most of our success with other people has less to do with being right than with how we make them feel. We like to believe that facts and good arguments carry the day. Carnegie spent his life watching that belief fail. When we deal with people, he says, we are not dealing with creatures of logic but with creatures of emotion, full of prejudices and moved by pride and vanity. A correct argument that wounds someone's pride usually loses. A modest request that leaves someone feeling respected usually wins.

He wrote the book from decades of teaching adults how to speak in public and deal with colleagues. In his evening courses he noticed that the engineers, salespeople and managers who enrolled needed something more than speaking skills. They needed the art of getting along with people at work, at home and in business, and no practical textbook for it existed. So he studied psychology and the biographies of leaders from Lincoln to Rockefeller, interviewed successful people, and turned his classes into a kind of laboratory, where students tried each principle during the week and reported back on what happened. The book is the distilled result of that testing.

Why does this matter so much? Carnegie cites research suggesting that even in technical fields like engineering, only a small share of financial success comes from technical knowledge, while the larger part comes from personality and the ability to deal with and lead people. He quotes John D. Rockefeller saying he would pay more for the ability to handle people than for any other ability under the sun. The point is not that competence is unimportant. It is that competence alone rarely moves anyone. Ability gets you into the room, and how people feel about you decides what happens there.

The book has four parts. The first gives three fundamental techniques for handling people. The second gives six ways to make people like you. The third, the longest, gives twelve ways to win people to your way of thinking. The fourth gives nine ways to change people as a leader without giving offense or arousing resentment. Beneath all thirty principles sit two observations about human nature. Everyone is mainly interested in themselves, and everyone deeply wants to feel important and appreciated. Nearly every principle is a way of honoring those two facts instead of fighting them.

It reads less like theory than like a set of habits to practice, and Carnegie insists on that. He opens with advice on how to use the book: develop a deep, driving desire to master these skills, read each chapter twice, stop often to ask yourself how you could apply each idea, review the book every month, and use the principles at every opportunity. He even suggests making a game of it by asking a friend or your spouse to catch you whenever you break a principle and paying a small penalty each time, and keeping a weekly record of where you slipped and where you succeeded. Knowing the principles changes nothing, he says. Only acting on them does, because these skills are learned the way swimming is learned, by doing.

## Three rules for handling people

The foundation is three rules for handling people, and the first is the hardest: do not criticize, condemn, or complain. Carnegie opens with an unsettling observation. A notorious gunman, cornered by police after a shooting, wrote a letter during the siege describing himself as a man with a weary but kind heart who would harm nobody. Al Capone saw himself as a misunderstood public benefactor. The warden of Sing Sing prison told Carnegie that few criminals regard themselves as bad people. If hardened criminals blame everyone but themselves, Carnegie asks, what about the ordinary people we deal with every day?

That is why criticism fails. It puts people on the defensive and makes them justify themselves. It wounds their pride and their sense of importance, stirs up resentment, and rarely produces the change we wanted. Carnegie points to B. F. Skinner's finding that animals rewarded for good behavior learn faster and retain more than animals punished for bad behavior, and says people work the same way. Criticism costs you goodwill and leaves a sting that can last for years. Lincoln learned this as a young man, when a mocking anonymous letter he wrote about a rival led to a challenge to a duel that was stopped only at the last minute. Later, after General Meade let Lee's army escape at Gettysburg, Lincoln wrote a furious rebuke and then never sent it, reasoning that in Meade's place he might have done the same. Carnegie also tells of the test pilot Bob Hoover, whose plane lost both engines because a mechanic filled it with the wrong fuel. After landing safely, Hoover did not berate the shaken mechanic. He asked that the same man service his plane the next day, to show confidence it would never happen again. Any fool can criticize, Carnegie says, and most fools do. It takes character to understand and forgive, so instead of condemning people, try to work out why they do what they do.

The second rule is to give honest and sincere appreciation. Carnegie calls this the big secret of dealing with people. The only way to get anyone to do anything is to make them want to do it, and one of the deepest human wants is the desire to feel important. John Dewey called it the desire to be important, and William James called the craving to be appreciated the deepest principle in human nature. Charles Schwab, one of the most highly paid executives of his era, said his real value was his ability to arouse enthusiasm in people, and his method was to be hearty in approbation and lavish in praise while avoiding criticism. Carnegie tells of a farm woman who, after years of cooking for men who never said a word of thanks, served them a heap of hay. We feed people's bodies, he notes, but rarely nourish their self-esteem.

Flattery is not the answer. Flattery is counterfeit appreciation. It is insincere and selfish and comes from the teeth out, while real appreciation comes from the heart, and people can usually tell the difference. The habit Carnegie recommends is to stop thinking about ourselves and look for the other person's genuine strengths. Emerson said every person he met was his superior in some way, and in that way he learned from them. Find that way, and say so honestly.

The third rule is to arouse in the other person an eager want. Carnegie loves strawberries and cream, but when he goes fishing he baits the hook with worms, because that is what fish want. Yet with people we keep talking about what you need, meaning our own needs and goals, which nobody else cares much about. Emerson and his son once pushed and pulled a stubborn calf toward the barn and got nowhere, until their housemaid put her finger in the calf's mouth and gently led it in. When a hotel tried to triple Carnegie's rent for a lecture ballroom, he wrote out the hotel's own gains and losses, pointing out that his lectures brought crowds of educated people into the building, and the increase was cut sharply. Henry Ford said the secret of success is getting the other person's point of view. So before any request, ask how you can make this person want to do it, and frame it around what the other person wants.

## Six ways to be liked

Carnegie's six ways to make people like you begin with becoming genuinely interested in other people. The dog, he notes, makes friends simply by being delighted to see you. You can make more friends in two months by becoming interested in others than in two years of trying to get them interested in you. Most people think about themselves nearly all the time, and the psychologist Alfred Adler wrote that the person who takes no interest in others has the greatest difficulties in life. Theodore Roosevelt greeted servants warmly by name, and once telephoned his valet's wife just to tell her that a bird she had asked about was outside her window. The magician Howard Thurston told himself before every show that he loved his audience. In practice, greet people with enthusiasm, ask about their lives, remember what matters to them, and do things for them that take time and thought.

Second, smile. Actions speak louder than words, and a smile says I like you and I am glad to see you. It has to be real, because a mechanical grin fools no one. Carnegie tells of a stockbroker who rarely smiled even at his wife, took on the challenge of smiling at everyone for a week, and found his home life and his business relationships transformed. If you don't feel like smiling, Carnegie draws on William James: action and feeling go together, so act cheerful and the feeling tends to follow. A smile even comes through on the phone.

Third, remember that a person's name is, to them, the sweetest and most important sound in any language. Using it is a subtle compliment, and forgetting or misspelling it is a small insult. Jim Farley, who helped Franklin Roosevelt reach the White House, was famous for remembering huge numbers of people by first name along with details about their families. Andrew Carnegie got neighborhood boys to gather food for his rabbits by promising to name the rabbits after them, and as an industrialist he named a steel works after a railroad president he wanted as a customer. The method is simple: catch the name clearly, repeat it, use it, and connect it to the person's face.

Fourth, be a good listener and encourage others to talk about themselves. At one dinner Carnegie spent hours listening to a botanist talk about exotic plants and hardly said a word, yet the botanist told the host that Carnegie was a most interesting conversationalist. Carnegie also describes a furious telephone customer who refused to pay and threatened to sue, until a company representative simply listened sympathetically through hours of complaints. The man paid and dropped his grievances, because what he really wanted was to feel important. To be interesting, be interested. Ask questions people enjoy answering.

Fifth, talk in terms of the other person's interests. Before meeting a visitor, Theodore Roosevelt would stay up reading about whatever that person cared about. A baker who had tried for years to sell bread to a hotel learned that the manager was devoted to a hotel association, asked him about it, and soon got a call asking for prices. The royal road to a person's heart is to talk about what they treasure most.

Sixth, make the other person feel important, sincerely. Carnegie once told a bored post office clerk that he wished he had the man's fine head of hair, and the clerk lit up. When a student later asked what Carnegie had wanted from him, he replied that he wanted nothing; he simply wanted to give a moment of happiness. Small courtesies like thank you and I'm sorry to trouble you do this every day. As Disraeli said, talk to people about themselves and they will listen for hours.

The thread running through all six is attention. Interest, a smile, a remembered name, listening, talk about their interests, and sincere recognition all tell someone that they matter. People are drawn to those who pay attention to them, and they drift away from those who only talk about themselves.

## Winning people over

The longest part of the book gives twelve ways to win people to your way of thinking, and it begins with a warning about argument: the only way to get the best of one is to avoid it. At a banquet, Carnegie corrected a guest who attributed a Shakespeare line to the Bible. A friend who was a Shakespeare scholar kicked him under the table and declared the guest right, then explained later that proving a man wrong in public would never make him like you. Nearly every argument leaves both sides more convinced they were right. Even when you win, you lose, because the other person feels humiliated and resents your triumph, and someone convinced against their will holds the same opinion still. So welcome disagreement, distrust your first defensive instinct, control your temper, listen first, look for points of agreement, and promise to think it over.

Second, show respect for the other person's opinions and never tell them flatly that they are wrong. That is an attack on their intelligence and pride, and it makes them dig in. Benjamin Franklin, told that his blunt contradicting made him unbearable, dropped words like certainly and began saying it appears to me at present, and found people far more open. Carnegie suggests saying, I may be wrong, I frequently am, so let's examine the facts. Third, if you are wrong, admit it quickly and emphatically. Caught by a park policeman with his dog off its leash, Carnegie condemned himself before the officer could, and the officer, given the chance to be generous, let him go. Saying the harsh things about yourself first leaves the other person nothing to attack and often makes them merciful.

Fourth, begin in a friendly way. Lincoln observed that a drop of honey catches more flies than a gallon of gall, and in Aesop's fable it is the sun, not the wind, that gets the traveler to remove his coat. When John D. Rockefeller Jr. faced bitter striking miners in Colorado, he opened with warmth and respect and won over men who had hated him. Fifth, get the other person saying yes early. Socrates built his arguments on questions his opponents had to agree with. Once someone says no, pride demands they stay consistent, but a series of yeses moves their mind in an accepting direction, so each small agreement makes a larger one easier.

Sixth, let the other person do most of the talking. People want to talk about their own achievements and problems, and interrupting to make your points only frustrates them. A salesman who had lost his voice let the client speak for him during the pitch and won the contract. If you want friends, let your friends excel you. Seventh, let the other person feel that the idea is theirs. People trust ideas they discover far more than ideas handed to them. A designer whose sketches had been rejected for years began bringing unfinished ones and asking the buyer how to complete them, and the buyer bought designs he had helped shape.

Eighth, try honestly to see things from the other person's point of view. People act for reasons that make sense to them, and finding those reasons is the key to their behavior. Carnegie used to scold boys for lighting fires in a park and got only sullen compliance. When he instead admitted he had loved building fires as a boy and explained the danger, they cooperated. Ninth, be sympathetic to the other person's ideas and desires, with a phrase like, I don't blame you for feeling as you do, and in your place I would feel the same. After a slip on a radio broadcast drew a furious letter from a listener, Carnegie phoned her and sympathized, and she ended up apologizing to him. Tenth, appeal to nobler motives. People have a real reason and a reason that sounds good, and appealing to honesty or fairness invites them to live up to it.

Eleventh, dramatize your ideas. Stating a truth is not enough. It must be made vivid and memorable, the way good showmen and advertisers do. Twelfth, when nothing else works, throw down a challenge. Charles Schwab, facing an underperforming mill, chalked the day shift's output on the floor. The night shift saw it and beat it, and the day shift then beat that. People like to prove what they can do, and the desire to excel is a powerful motive.

## Correcting without offense

The final part is about being a leader, which Carnegie defines as changing people's behavior without giving offense or arousing resentment. The first principle is to begin with praise and honest appreciation. We hear unpleasant things more easily after hearing something good about ourselves, the way a barber lathers a man before he shaves him. Calvin Coolidge once told his secretary that her dress was lovely, then asked her to be more careful with her punctuation.

Second, call attention to people's mistakes indirectly. Charles Schwab once found workers smoking right under a No Smoking sign. Instead of pointing to the sign, he handed each man a cigar and said he'd appreciate it if they smoked these outside. They knew they had broken the rule, and they admired him for not rubbing it in. Carnegie also suggests a small change in wording. Many people praise and then say but, which makes the praise sound like a setup for criticism. Use and instead. Rather than saying we're proud of your grades, but your algebra needs work, say we're proud of your grades, and if you keep it up your algebra will rise too.

Third, talk about your own mistakes before criticizing the other person. When Carnegie's young niece made errors as his secretary, he reminded himself that he had made worse mistakes at her age and began his corrections by admitting them. Criticism is far easier to accept from someone who admits they are not perfect. Fourth, ask questions instead of giving direct orders. Owen D. Young, who led General Electric, was said never to give orders. He would say you might consider this, or do you think this would work? A factory manager facing a nearly impossible deadline explained the situation to his workers and asked whether there was any way to handle it. They came up with ideas and met the deadline, because the plan was theirs. Questions preserve dignity and make people more willing to accept the decision.

Fifth, let the other person save face. General Electric once needed to remove the brilliant engineer Charles Steinmetz as head of a department he was poorly suited to run. Instead of humiliating him, the company gave him a new title, consulting engineer, and let someone else run the department, and he was pleased. Even when we are right and the other person is plainly wrong, making them lose face only damages them and our relationship. Carnegie quotes Antoine de Saint-Exupéry: I have no right to say or do anything that diminishes a man in his own eyes.

Sixth, praise the slightest improvement and praise every improvement. Be hearty in your approbation and lavish in your praise. Animal trainers reward each small step forward, and people respond the same way. The great singer Enrico Caruso was told as a boy that he could not sing, but his mother believed in him and praised every bit of progress. Praise should be specific, which makes it believable and shows you really noticed. Seventh, give the other person a fine reputation to live up to. If you want someone to improve in some way, act as though that quality were already one of their strengths. Shakespeare advised, assume a virtue if you have it not. A service manager whose once excellent mechanic had grown sloppy told him he had a reputation as a fine mechanic and asked for help getting his work back to that standard, and the mechanic rose to it.

Eighth, use encouragement and make the fault seem easy to correct. Tell a child or an employee that they are hopeless at something and you destroy their incentive to try. Carnegie was convinced in middle age that he could never learn bridge, until his friend Lowell Thomas told him it was only memory and judgment and that he had a natural flair for it. He learned. Ninth, make the other person happy about doing the thing you suggest. Napoleon handed out medals and grand titles, knowing that people respond to recognition. When Woodrow Wilson chose Colonel House instead of his Secretary of State for a peace mission, House told the secretary that he was too important a figure to go without attracting attention, so the man felt honored rather than snubbed. Before you ask, be sincere, know exactly what you want, and present it so the other person sees the benefit to themselves.

## Why it works

Critics sometimes read the book as a manual for manipulation, a bag of tricks for getting your way by pretending to care. Carnegie saw that objection coming and keeps returning to sincerity. He says plainly that he is not teaching flattery, that people see through counterfeit warmth quickly, and that if you use these principles merely to get something out of people, they will fail. His post office story makes the point. The compliment worked precisely because he wanted nothing back.

The techniques only work when you actually care about the person in front of you. That is not a moral add-on; it is the mechanism. Invented appreciation sounds hollow, listening used as a tactic drifts, and a forced smile looks like a smirk. What makes the principles effective is that they turn your attention away from yourself and onto the other person, which is exactly what people crave and rarely receive.

Read that way, the book is less about winning and more about a daily discipline of curiosity, generosity, and restraint. Curiosity drives genuine interest, listening and seeing things from the other person's point of view. Generosity shows up as appreciation, praise for small improvements, a fine reputation to live up to, and letting others take credit for ideas. Restraint is the hardest of the three: holding back criticism, giving up the urge to win an argument, never telling someone flatly that they are wrong, and letting them save face. The parts also connect. Avoiding criticism and arguments protects people's pride. Appreciation and attention feed their need to feel important. Framing requests around their wants then turns that goodwill into action.

Carnegie's original editions even applied the same rules to home life, with chapters arguing that nagging, criticism and trying to remake a spouse destroy marriages, while appreciation, small attentions and ordinary courtesy sustain them. He pointed to famous marriages, including those of Tolstoy and Lincoln, that were made miserable by constant complaint. The lesson is the same as at work: the people closest to us need appreciation most and often get it least.

Nearly a century later, the advice holds up because the underlying facts have not changed. Workplaces and technology have been transformed, but people still want to be heard, respected, and appreciated, and they still resist being criticized, contradicted in public or ordered around. The person who provides attention and appreciation consistently, and who treats being right as less important than keeping goodwill, ends up with influence they never had to demand. That is Carnegie's deepest lesson. Influence is not taken from people. It is given by them, to those who make them feel valued.
