## Three revolutions

Yuval Noah Harari opens with a puzzle that sounds simple and turns out to be deep. How did one species of ape, not the strongest, not the fastest, and for most of its existence not very important, come to dominate the planet? Today Homo sapiens decides the fate of nearly every other living thing. Yet for hundreds of thousands of years our ancestors were what Harari calls an animal of no significance, with no more effect on the world around them than baboons or hyenas. The book is his attempt to explain that transformation, and to ask whether it was actually good for us.

He begins by reminding us that we were not always alone. The word human properly refers to the whole genus Homo, and for most of the last two million years several human species lived at the same time. Neanderthals, stocky and built for Ice Age cold, lived in Europe and western Asia. Homo erectus survived in East Asia for close to two million years, far longer than we have existed. There were dwarf humans on the Indonesian island of Flores and the Denisovans in Siberia. Sapiens evolved in East Africa as just one branch among many. Our feeling that we are the only humans is a recent illusion, created by the fact that all our siblings disappeared.

Why did they vanish? Harari describes two theories. The interbreeding theory says sapiens mixed with the others until the populations merged, and genetic evidence shows that many people today carry a small share of Neanderthal DNA. The replacement theory says sapiens outcompeted or killed them. Harari thinks both played a part, but stresses that wherever sapiens arrived, the other humans soon disappeared. That suggests sapiens had some new advantage, and it was not the body. Large brains devour about a quarter of our energy at rest. Walking upright strained backs and narrowed the birth canal, so human babies are born early and helpless and must be raised by a group. Fire let humans cook, which made food easier to digest and allowed smaller guts. Still, for most of their history humans lived in the middle of the food chain, scavenging what lions and hyenas left behind. Our jump to the top came so suddenly that, Harari argues, neither we nor the ecosystem had time to adjust, which helps explain why we are such an anxious and dangerous species.

So what was the new advantage? Harari organizes the whole story around three revolutions. The Cognitive Revolution, around seventy thousand years ago, gave sapiens a new kind of thinking and language, and with it the ability to believe in shared stories. The Agricultural Revolution, beginning about twelve thousand years ago, tied people to fields and villages, multiplied their numbers, and gave rise to kingdoms, writing and money. Between these and the modern era, he traces how thousands of separate cultures gradually merged into a single global civilization.

The Scientific Revolution, which began only about five hundred years ago, may end history as we know it and start something entirely different. It began when people admitted how much they did not know, and gained momentum when governments and businesses discovered that research could turn knowledge into power. Throughout the book, Harari keeps asking questions that standard histories skip: did each revolution make individual people happier, and what did it cost other animals? He also draws a sharp line between biology and history. Biology sets the limits of what sapiens can do, but within those limits, culture, built from the stories we tell, is what changes. From the Cognitive Revolution on, history rather than genetics became the main engine of change for our species.

## Shared stories

About seventy thousand years ago something changed. Sapiens began spreading out of Africa and soon built boats, oil lamps, bows and arrows and needles for sewing warm clothes, and started making art. Harari calls this the Cognitive Revolution. He suggests that chance genetic mutations may have changed the wiring of the brain, though no one knows exactly how. What matters is the result: a new way of thinking and communicating. Many animals have language. Some monkeys have distinct calls that mean, roughly, careful, an eagle, or careful, a lion. Sapiens language was far more flexible, able to combine a limited number of sounds into an endless variety of sentences.

Harari considers why that mattered. One theory is that it let people share detailed information, for instance that there are lions by the river, where they were seen and which way they went. A second is the gossip theory: survival depended on knowing who in the band was trustworthy, who was lazy and who was a cheat, and gossip lets a group of up to about a hundred and fifty people hold together. But the truly unique feature of our language, Harari argues, is the ability to talk about things that do not exist. A monkey can warn of a lion. Only a human can say that the lion is the guardian spirit of the tribe. An ancient ivory figurine from a German cave, with a man's body and a lion's head, is among the earliest evidence of this kind of imagination. Gods, nations, money, laws and human rights are fictions of the same type, which Harari calls imagined orders. They exist only in the shared imagination of many people.

Why is fiction so powerful? Because it lets us cooperate flexibly in large numbers. Chimpanzees can cooperate, but only in small groups whose members know each other intimately through grooming, alliances and fights for rank. When a troop grows too large, it splits. Ants and bees cooperate in huge numbers, but rigidly, following genetic instructions. Sapiens alone can cooperate flexibly with countless strangers, in the millions, as long as they believe the same story. Two Catholics who have never met can go on pilgrimage together, two lawyers who are strangers can defend a client because both believe in law and justice, and two traders on different continents can do business because both trust the dollar. None of these things exists outside our shared beliefs.

Harari's favorite example is the limited liability company. Peugeot, the French car maker, began as a small family workshop and grew into a giant employing many thousands. But where exactly is Peugeot? It is not its cars, its factories, its workers or its managers. If they all disappeared, the company could borrow money and rebuild. If a judge ordered it dissolved, it would cease to exist even with every factory standing. Peugeot exists because lawyers performed rituals of writing documents and signing them, and because society believes in the story of corporate law. Such a legal entity can own property, take loans, be sued, and outlive its founders, while protecting them from personal ruin if it fails.

This had a profound consequence. Once behavior depended on stories rather than genes, sapiens could change rapidly. Chimpanzee social structure is fixed by biology and would take many generations to change. Humans can change their social order by changing the story, as when the French Revolution replaced belief in the divine right of kings with belief in the sovereignty of the people almost overnight, or when an entire class of Catholic priests gives up having children because of a shared belief. From this point, history outruns biology.

Harari then describes how foragers lived. They moved in small bands, ate a varied diet, worked fewer hours than later farmers, and suffered less from infectious disease. The average forager had wider skills and deeper knowledge of her surroundings than most modern people. Their beliefs were probably animist, though he admits we know very little about them. But they were not gentle guardians of nature. Wherever sapiens arrived, first in Australia and later in the Americas, most of the large animals soon vanished, from giant marsupials to mammoths and ground sloths. Through hunting and burning, foragers drove much of the world's megafauna extinct long before writing, which leads Harari to call sapiens an ecological serial killer.

## The wheat bargain

The second part of the book tackles the Agricultural Revolution, and Harari's verdict is deliberately shocking: he calls it history's biggest fraud. The usual story says farming brought security, comfort and leisure. Harari argues almost the opposite. Starting about twelve thousand years ago, independently in places such as the Middle East, China and Central America, people began devoting their days to manipulating a handful of plant and animal species. What they gained was more calories per acre. What they lost was much of what had made life good.

Farming meant harder work, from dawn to dusk, clearing stones, hauling water, weeding and guarding fields. Ancient skeletons show slipped discs, arthritis and hernias, because the human body evolved for climbing and running, not for stooping over crops. Diets narrowed to a single starch, poorer in vitamins and minerals. Crowding together with animals bred infectious disease. Dependence on one or two crops meant that a drought, blight or raid could bring famine. Yet the population exploded, because a field of wheat feeds far more people than the same land left wild. That is Harari's central distinction. Measured by the number of DNA copies, farming was a triumph for the species. For the individual, it was often a step down from the varied, leisurely life of a forager. Evolution measures success in numbers, not in happiness.

This is why he says it makes sense to think that wheat domesticated us, rather than the reverse. Wheat was once a wild grass confined to a small part of the Middle East. Today it covers vast areas of the globe, because it got sapiens to clear forests, pick out rocks, carry water and fight off pests on its behalf. The word domesticate comes from the Latin for house, and it was humans who ended up living in permanent houses beside their fields. Why did anyone accept the deal? Harari's answer is the luxury trap. No one ever decided to transform human life. Each small step, such as tending wild wheat a little longer or settling near a good harvest, made sense at the time. Extra food meant more children, more children meant more mouths, and soon there was no way back to foraging. People seeking an easier life built a harder one, a pattern Harari sees again in labor-saving technologies like email that only make us busier.

Domesticated animals paid an even steeper price. Chickens, cattle, pigs and sheep became some of the most widespread large animals on Earth, an evolutionary success, yet individual animals were confined, castrated, separated from their young and slaughtered. Farming also changed how people thought about time. Foragers lived largely in the present, but farmers had to worry constantly about next season's rain and harvest. That anxiety, and the new ability to store surplus grain, laid the foundations of politics. Elites seized the surplus from peasants, and it fed priests, soldiers, and kings, and officials who never farmed. Most peasants worked harder only to support a small privileged class.

To organize millions of strangers, farming societies needed bigger myths, the imagined orders of empire. Harari compares the Code of Hammurabi, which declared that the gods had divided Babylonian society into superior people, commoners and slaves, with the American Declaration of Independence, which declared that all men are created equal. Both felt like natural truths to their believers, and both, biologically speaking, are fictions. Humans were not created; they evolved, and evolution gives no one equal traits or inalienable rights. Imagined orders survive because people believe in them, and that belief is built into architecture, education, laws and even our desires, so that what we want is shaped by the order we live in. Harari calls this intersubjective reality: not objective like gravity, not merely personal, but real in the linked minds of many.

Large societies also faced an information problem: tracking taxes, debts and property for millions. The Sumerians solved it by inventing writing, first as a partial script for numbers and accounts, which made bureaucracy and archives possible. Every imagined order also produced hierarchies, from caste in India to slavery and racial division in America to male dominance almost everywhere. Harari argues these usually begin in historical accident and persist through vicious circles: a group shut out from wealth and education becomes poor, and its poverty is taken as proof of inferiority. Patriarchy puzzles him most, and he finds explanations based on muscle or aggression unconvincing.

## What unites us

The third part of the book steps back to find a pattern. Cultures are never fixed. They keep changing, partly because each contains contradictions, such as Christian humility against the knightly code of honor, or liberty against equality in modern politics. Over thousands of years, the overall direction of change has been toward unity. Countless isolated little worlds merged into fewer and larger civilizations, and today almost every human lives within a single global system of politics, trade and science. Harari credits three universal orders, each of which imagined the whole world as one potential community: the economic order of merchants, the political order of conquerors, and the religious order of prophets.

Money is the first and most successful. Harari stresses that it is not a material reality but a psychological one, a system of mutual trust. People have used barley, silver measured by weight, coins stamped with a ruler's guarantee, paper notes, and now mostly numbers stored in computers. In prisons and prisoner of war camps, cigarettes have served as money, even for non-smokers. Barter is hopelessly clumsy, because each trade requires finding someone who wants exactly what you have. Money lets anything be converted into anything, stored and moved. Above all, it lets strangers who share no language, god or king cooperate. Christian and Muslim merchants trusted each other's gold even during holy wars, and Roman coins were accepted in India, far beyond Roman rule. In his words, money is the most universal and most efficient system of mutual trust ever devised. But it has a dark side: when everything can be bought, money can corrode family loyalty, community and other values that should not be for sale.

Empires are the second unifier. Harari defines an empire as a political order that rules many distinct peoples, each with its own identity, and that has flexible borders and an appetite for more. Empires have been the world's most common form of government for about two and a half thousand years, and surprisingly stable. They are rightly remembered for conquest, slavery and exploitation. Yet they also melted small cultures into larger ones, spreading languages, laws, roads, art and ideas, and they often claimed to rule for the good of everyone. Over generations, conquered peoples adopted the imperial culture and then demanded equal standing within it, until the line between conquerors and conquered faded. Most cultures today are imperial legacies. An Indian nationalist who wanted to reject everything left by the British Empire would have to give up parliamentary democracy, the English language, the railways and even cricket, and by the same logic the Taj Mahal, built by a foreign Muslim dynasty.

Universal religions are the third unifier. Harari defines religion as a system of human norms and values founded on belief in a superhuman order. Early animism was local, tied to particular places and spirits. Farming brought polytheism, with gods of rain, fertility and war, and polytheism was usually tolerant, since adding another god cost little. The Romans persecuted Christians only sporadically. Monotheism claimed one universal god and one truth for everyone, so it became missionary and often violent, and Christians killed far more fellow Christians in their religious wars than pagan Rome ever did. Monotheism also quietly absorbed older ideas, from saints who resembled minor gods to dualist beliefs in a cosmic battle between good and evil. Harari also counts Buddhism, which teaches that suffering comes from craving, and he argues that modern creeds such as liberalism, communism, capitalism and nationalism are religions too, because they rest on belief in a superhuman order of natural laws or sacred human qualities. Humanism, the worship of humanity itself, came in liberal, socialist and evolutionary versions, the last of which inspired the Nazis.

Harari insists that these unifiers were neither purely good nor purely evil. Money built trust and eroded communities; empires brought massacres and the common languages we now share; religions brought both compassion and holy war. The people who lived under them rarely had a choice. He also warns against hindsight. History looks inevitable afterward, but it is a chaotic system that reacts to predictions about itself, so it cannot be forecast. Nor does it bend toward human well-being; cultures can spread like mental parasites even when they harm their hosts. We study history not to predict the future but to see that our world was never the only possible one.

## Admitting ignorance

The final part of the book covers the last five hundred years, in which human population, production and energy use grew beyond anything before. Harari argues that the Scientific Revolution began with a confession: we do not know. Earlier traditions, whether Christian, Muslim, Buddhist or Confucian, assumed that everything important about the world was already known, preserved in scripture or ancient wisdom. If the sacred texts did not mention something, it was probably not worth knowing. Modern science starts from the opposite premise, the admission that we are ignorant, even about our most cherished theories, and that new evidence may overturn anything.

He identifies three features of modern science. First, the willingness to admit ignorance. Second, the central role of observation and mathematics: gathering data, then using mathematical tools to connect observations into general theories. Third, the use of those theories to gain new powers, above all new technologies. Science is not pure curiosity; it is a project for making people more powerful. Maps capture the shift. Medieval maps filled every space with familiar or imaginary lands, while European maps from the fifteenth century began leaving blank spaces, an open invitation to explore. Admitting ignorance also produced the idea of progress. If we can discover what we do not know, then poverty, disease and perhaps even death become technical problems to be solved, an ambition Harari calls the Gilgamesh Project.

Yet curiosity alone does not explain why science flourished where it did. Research costs money, and someone decides what to fund. Harari's answer is a feedback loop among science, empire and capital. Rulers and businessmen funded research because it promised power and profit, and the resulting knowledge produced more power and profit, which paid for more research. Around 1500, Europe was not technologically ahead of China or the Muslim world. What set Europeans apart was a mindset shared by scientists and conquerors alike: admit ignorance, then go out and look. Explorers sailed with scientists on board. James Cook's voyage to the Pacific carried astronomers and botanists, helped find a way to prevent scurvy, and paved the way for the British conquest of Australia and New Zealand, with catastrophic results for their native peoples. Napoleon took scholars with him when he invaded Egypt. Empires gathered knowledge of languages, plants and peoples, and science in turn supplied tools and justifications for imperial rule, including later racist theories.

The third partner was capital. For most of history, Harari explains, economies barely grew, so lending was rare and suspect, because if the pie never grows, one person's gain is another's loss. Credit is a bet on the future. Once people came to believe, largely thanks to science, that the future would be richer than the present, they were willing to lend. Loans financed new businesses, the economy grew, and growth justified more lending. Adam Smith added a revolutionary moral idea: a person who seeks profit and reinvests it enriches everyone, so self-interest can serve the common good. The central rule of capitalism became that profits must be reinvested to produce more growth.

This is how Investors funded empire. Columbus spent years seeking backers before the Spanish crown gambled on him, and the gold and silver of the Americas repaid the bet many times over. Later, joint-stock companies such as the Dutch East India Company pooled money from many shareholders to conquer and trade, ruling much of Indonesia, while its British counterpart conquered India. The small Dutch Republic outcompeted mighty Spain partly because it repaid its debts and protected property, which made it a safer place to lend. France, by contrast, saw its credit collapse after a speculative bubble, a blow that fed the financial crisis behind the French Revolution. Capitalism also has a dark side. Britain fought the Opium Wars to keep selling drugs to China, and the Atlantic slave trade was run largely by profit-seeking companies. Capitalism, for Harari, is as much a faith as an economic system, and its central article of faith is trust in growth.

## Power, but happiness?

The Industrial Revolution, Harari explains, was above all a revolution in energy conversion. For most of history, almost all usable energy came from plants, through the muscles of people and animals. The steam engine showed that heat could be turned into motion, and soon coal, oil and electricity unlocked energy far beyond muscle power. With abundant energy came abundant raw materials and goods. The new problem was not scarcity but who would buy everything, and the answer was consumerism, an ethic that tells people that buying things is good for them and for the economy. Industrial farming also brought hidden misery to billions of animals, treated as production machines though they keep the emotional needs of their wild ancestors.

Industry reorganized time. Peasants worked by the seasons and the sun, and each town kept its own local time. Factories and railway timetables demanded precision, so nations standardized their clocks, and life came to run on schedules, from school bells to news broadcasts. Even more deeply, industry dismantled the oldest pillars of human life, the family and the local community. For millions of years people depended on relatives and neighbors for food, care, education, work and protection. Now the state and the market offered all of these on one condition: become individuals. Marry whoever you like, move wherever the jobs are, let the state educate your children and the market feed you. This freed many people from oppressive families, but left them reliant on impersonal bureaucracies and businesses. In place of real communities, modern people belong to imagined communities such as nations and consumer tribes, millions of strangers who feel a shared identity. Harari adds that, despite its wars, the modern era has been unusually peaceful, because nuclear weapons made great power war suicidal, wealth now lies in knowledge that cannot be seized, and global elites see peace as profitable.

Then comes the question historians rarely ask: has any of this made people happier? Harari calls it the biggest gap in our understanding of history. If we are no happier than foragers or medieval peasants, what was all this for? He finds the evidence thin, but suggestive. Studies of well-being suggest that money raises happiness only up to a point, and that family and community matter more than wealth. A central finding is that happiness depends less on objective conditions than on the gap between conditions and expectations. When conditions improve, expectations rise, so a person with antibiotics and supermarkets may feel no more content than an ancestor without them. Mass media make it worse by comparing us constantly with the richest and most beautiful people on Earth.

Biologists add that happiness is governed by brain chemistry. Evolution set our emotional thermostat to keep us striving, never permanently satisfied, so most people drift back to a familiar range after both triumphs and disasters. If so, history barely matters for happiness, and the shortcut would be chemistry rather than revolutions. Others argue that happiness lies in seeing one's life as meaningful, and a medieval peasant who believed in heaven may have found more meaning than many modern workers. Harari suggests all such meaning may be a shared delusion, but a comforting one. Buddhism goes further: suffering comes from chasing pleasant feelings, and peace comes from ceasing to crave them.

Harari's final chapter looks ahead. For four billion years, life evolved by natural selection. Now sapiens is beginning to replace it with intelligent design. Through genetic engineering, scientists have already created a rabbit that glows green, and they hope to revive extinct species and perhaps redesign humans. Cyborg engineering links bodies and brains to machines, and with artificial intelligence and self-replicating computer programs, life might one day escape organic matter altogether. Defeating death has become a serious research goal. Within a few generations, sapiens may be replaced by beings as different from us as we are from Neanderthals. The ape that invented gods is on the verge of becoming one, yet we remain restless and irresponsible, seeking comfort and amusement without knowing where we are heading. The real question, Harari concludes, is not what we can become but what we will want to become, and perhaps what we want to want.
