## Deep vs. shallow

Cal Newport opens with the psychiatrist Carl Jung, who built a simple stone tower in the village of Bollingen, on the shore of a lake outside Zurich, and retreated there to think and write without interruption. Jung had a busy practice in the city, but the work that changed his field was done in that tower. Newport gives this kind of effort a name: deep work. He defines it as professional activity performed in a state of distraction-free concentration that pushes your abilities to their limit. Deep work creates new value, improves your skills, and produces results that are hard for others to copy.

Shallow work is the opposite. It is the logistical, cognitively undemanding work that fills most modern days: answering routine email, scheduling meetings, filling in forms, skimming feeds and chat threads. It is often done while distracted, it is easily replicated by almost anyone, and it creates little new value in the world. Newport is not saying shallow work is evil or that you can avoid it entirely. Some of it is necessary to keep a job running. His point is that it is cheap, and a career built mostly on it is fragile, because anything easy to replicate can be outsourced, automated, or done by someone else for less.

From these definitions comes the central claim of the book, which Newport calls the deep work hypothesis. The ability to do deep work is becoming more valuable in our economy at exactly the same time it is becoming rarer. Networked tools such as email, messaging and social media have made distraction constant, and writers like Nicholas Carr have argued that the internet is training our brains for skimming rather than sustained thought. Meanwhile, the work that the economy rewards most increasingly requires exactly the kind of concentration those tools erode.

When something is both valuable and scarce, the people who have it gain an outsized advantage. That is the practical promise of the book. If you cultivate the ability to concentrate deeply while most of your peers drift into constant connectivity, you will learn faster, produce better work, and stand out. Newport himself is an example: as a young computer science professor he wrote books and academic papers at a steady pace while avoiding social media and generally finishing his workday by early evening.

The book has two parts. Part one makes the case in three chapters, arguing that deep work is valuable, that it is rare, and that it is meaningful. Part two is a training program of four rules: work deeply, embrace boredom, quit social media, and drain the shallows. Together they are meant to move you from understanding why depth matters to actually rebuilding your days around it.

## Output equals time times focus

Why is deep work so valuable? Newport starts with the economists Erik Brynjolfsson and Andrew McAfee, who argue that intelligent machines are reshaping the labor market in what they call the Great Restructuring. Newport describes three groups positioned to win. The first are people who can work well with complex machines and technology, like the data analyst Nate Silver. The second are superstars, top performers in fields where technology lets the best person serve a global market, so employers hire the very best rather than settling for someone local. The third are owners with capital to invest in new technology. Since most of us cannot simply become owners, the question is how to join the first two groups.

His answer is two core abilities. First, you must be able to quickly master hard things, because the tools and skills that matter keep changing. Second, you must be able to produce at an elite level, in both quality and speed. If you cannot learn, you cannot keep up, and if you cannot produce, your talent never turns into results anyone can see. Newport argues that both abilities depend on sustained focus.

Learning hard things requires what the psychologist Anders Ericsson called deliberate practice. Deliberate practice means focusing your attention tightly on the specific skill you are trying to improve and getting feedback so you can correct your approach. Newport adds a neurological explanation: intense focus on a single skill causes the relevant brain circuits to fire repeatedly in isolation, which helps build myelin, the insulation that makes those circuits fire faster and more reliably. You cannot get that benefit while half-attending to a chat window or a phone. Distracted practice fires many circuits at once and strengthens none of them well.

For producing at an elite level, Newport offers a simple law: high-quality work produced equals time spent multiplied by intensity of focus. This explains why some people accomplish so much in so few hours. The organizational psychologist Adam Grant, for example, became one of the most prolific scholars in his field by batching his hardest work into long, uninterrupted stretches. If you raise the intensity, you can get more done in less time, and if you constantly fragment your attention, even long hours produce mediocre results.

The enemy of intensity is a phenomenon the business researcher Sophie Leroy calls attention residue. When you switch from one task to another, part of your attention stays stuck on the first task, and your performance on the second suffers. This happens even with brief switches. A quick glance at email in the middle of a demanding task feels harmless, but the unread message and the half-processed thoughts linger. The attention residue lowers the intensity of whatever you return to, and a day full of such glances means you never work at full capacity.

Newport is honest about exceptions. Some people succeed without much deep work, and he mentions Jack Dorsey, who ran two major companies while spending his days moving between meetings and constant interruptions. For certain executive roles, rapid decisions and availability are the job. But Newport argues these cases are unusual, and for most knowledge workers depth is what creates value.

## Why deep work is rare

If deep work is so valuable, why do so few organizations support it? Newport points to three trends. Open offices are meant to encourage collaboration and serendipitous encounters. Instant messaging promises fast communication. And many professionals are pushed to maintain a social media presence. Each of these sounds reasonable, and each pulls attention away from concentrated work. Together they create a culture of constant availability, where everyone is expected to respond quickly to everyone else.

Part of the explanation is what Newport calls the metric black hole. The costs of distraction are real but hard to measure. When a company adopts a new chat tool, nobody can easily put a number on how much deep thinking it destroyed, so the cost never shows up in a report. One executive at a media company tried to estimate the total time his employees spent on email and found the cost was startlingly large, but such calculations are rare. Because the damage stays invisible, businesses keep choosing tools and habits that seem convenient.

Into that gap steps the principle of least resistance. In the absence of clear feedback about what helps or hurts, people tend toward the behaviors that are easiest in the moment. A culture where everyone answers instantly lets you get quick answers without planning ahead, so it persists even if it wrecks focus. Newport describes an experiment by the Harvard Business School professor Leslie Perlow with a team at the Boston Consulting Group. Each consultant was given scheduled time completely disconnected from the office. The consultants resisted at first, but they ended up more satisfied, communicating better, and producing better work. The constant connectivity had never been necessary, only easy.

A second escape from the metric black hole is busyness as a proxy for productivity. Knowledge work is hard to measure, so people fall back on what is visible: answering messages fast, attending meetings, and generally looking responsive. Visibly doing lots of shallow things becomes a way to signal value, even when it produces little. Newport also describes a cultural belief he calls the cult of the internet, borrowing from the critic Neil Postman. Anything connected to the internet is assumed to be innovative and good, so questioning a new tool sounds backward rather than prudent.

There is also a personal side. Distraction feels good. Every new message offers a small hit of novelty, and research on desire suggests people spend much of their day resisting urges, with the pull of media and communication among the hardest to resist. Willpower alone keeps losing. That is why Newport insists that deep work requires a deliberate structure rather than good intentions.

Newport ends this argument with a twist. The fact that deep work is undervalued is bad for many businesses, but it is an opportunity for you. If most people are drifting into shallow habits, the ones who resist will be scarce, and scarcity is what makes a skill valuable.

## Rule 1 · Work deeply

Part two turns to practice, and rule one is to work deeply. Because willpower is a limited resource that gets used up as you resist distractions through the day, the key is not to rely on it. Instead, build rituals and routines that make deep work automatic, so you spend as little willpower as possible getting into a focused state.

Start by choosing a depth philosophy that fits your job. The monastic approach eliminates or radically minimizes shallow obligations. The computer scientist Donald Knuth famously does not use email, because his work requires long periods of uninterrupted study. This suits people whose value comes from one clear, deep output. The bimodal approach divides your time, dedicating clearly defined stretches to depth and leaving the rest open. Jung retreated to his tower for long spells, and Adam Grant concentrates his teaching into one semester so he can focus on research in the other, sometimes closing his door to all contact for several days at a time. A deep stretch should be at least a full day. The rhythmic approach turns depth into a regular habit, blocking the same hours every day, for example the early morning, and marking each successful day on a calendar so that you are motivated not to break the chain. This suits most people with ordinary jobs. Finally, the journalistic approach fits deep work into whatever free time appears, the way the journalist Walter Isaacson wrote books in spare moments. Newport warns it only works once you are already good at switching quickly into deep focus.

Next, ritualize. Great thinkers often had strict habits around their work, and Newport suggests your ritual answer a few questions. Decide on a specific location and time, and how long the session will last. Then decide how you will work once you start, defining rules such as no internet at all, or a target like a certain number of words per stretch of time. Finally, decide how you will support your work, whether with coffee, the right food and movement such as a walk, or having all your materials ready so the body and the setting do not interrupt you.

Sometimes a bigger push helps. Newport calls this the grand gesture: a radical change of environment or a significant investment of money or effort to dedicate yourself to a task. J.K. Rowling checked into a luxury hotel to finish the final Harry Potter book. Bill Gates took think weeks, retreating to a cabin with stacks of reading. The writer Peter Shankman booked a round-trip flight to Tokyo just to write on the plane. The cost raises the perceived importance of the work, which strengthens your motivation.

Depth does not mean isolation. Newport separates serendipitous collaboration from deep collaboration. Places like Bell Labs and MIT's famous Building 20 mixed people in ways that sparked ideas, but the real work happened when individuals or pairs went off to concentrate. He recommends a hub-and-spoke model: expose yourself to ideas in the hub, then retreat to a spoke to work. Working side by side with someone on a hard problem, which he calls the whiteboard effect, can even push you deeper than working alone.

To actually get deep work done, execute like a business. Newport borrows the four disciplines of execution. Focus on a small number of wildly important goals, which generate real enthusiasm. Act on lead measures, the behaviors that drive results, and for deep work the best lead measure is simply hours spent in deep work on those goals. Keep a visible scoreboard, such as a card where you tally deep hours. And create a cadence of accountability with a weekly review of what went well.

Finally, be lazy. Downtime helps insight, because the unconscious mind is good at complex problems. It restores the attention you need, which is why a walk in nature refreshes you. And evening work is rarely important, because people can sustain only a few hours of real depth a day. So end each day with a shutdown ritual: check your task list and calendar, make sure every unfinished task has a plan, and then say a set phrase marking the day as done. Research suggests that making a plan for unfinished tasks quiets the nagging thoughts that would otherwise follow you home.

## Rule 2 · Embrace boredom

Rule two starts from a simple claim: the ability to concentrate is a skill that has to be trained, not a trait you either have or lack. And like any skill, it can also decline. Newport cites research by the Stanford professor Clifford Nass suggesting that people who constantly switch attention among media struggle to filter out irrelevant information, even when they try to focus. If every moment of boredom, waiting in line or sitting at a red light, is a cue to reach for your phone, you are training your brain to need novelty. Then when you sit down to do deep work, your mind keeps seeking the stimulation it is used to.

The usual response is a digital detox, a week or a month off the internet. Newport argues this misses the point. Taking an occasional break from distraction does little if the rest of your time is spent in constant distraction. Instead, he says, you should schedule breaks from focus. Decide in advance the specific times you are allowed to use the internet, write down when the next block begins, and stay offline the rest of the time. Keep a notepad near your computer so that when you are tempted to look something up, you note it for the next online block instead.

Several details make this work. It holds even if your job needs heavy internet use; you just have more and larger online blocks. The essential rule is that the time outside the blocks must be completely free of internet use, because giving in even briefly teaches your brain that the urge pays off. And it helps to do the same at home, since evenings spent on a phone undo the training of the workday. The goal is to rewire your brain so it can tolerate the absence of novelty.

Newport also recommends working like Teddy Roosevelt. As a Harvard student, Roosevelt filled his days with activities, but he studied in short bursts of extreme intensity. You can copy this. Pick a deep task, estimate how long it would normally take, then set a hard deadline well below that and work with total concentration to meet it. These sprints stretch your capacity for intensity the way interval training stretches fitness.

Another tool is productive meditation. Take a period when you are busy physically but not mentally, such as walking, jogging, driving or showering, and focus your attention on a single well-defined professional problem. When your mind wanders, bring it back. Beware of looping, going over the same obvious points again and again. To avoid it, structure your thinking: first review the key variables of the problem, then define the specific next question you need to answer, and once you have an answer, consolidate it before moving on. Like meditation, this builds the mental muscles of focus, and it also turns idle time into useful time.

Finally, Newport suggests memory training, such as learning to memorize a deck of cards with techniques used by memory champions. These methods, which place images along a familiar route in your imagination, demand intense, sustained attention. Practicing them improves your general ability to concentrate, which carries over into deep work.

## Rule 3 · Quit social media

Rule three is to quit social media, but Newport's real target is broader: the habit of using tools by default. Network tools like social media, news sites and other online services fragment our time and weaken our ability to concentrate, yet most people adopt them without much thought. Newport describes the writer Baratunde Thurston, who was deeply connected online and decided to unplug for a while. He found the experience pleasant and the world did not end.

The problem is what Newport calls the any-benefit approach. People justify a tool if they can identify any possible benefit, or anything they might miss without it. Someone might stay on a platform because an old friend occasionally posts there, or because it might one day bring a business contact. By that standard, almost any tool qualifies, and the hidden costs in time and attention are ignored.

Newport proposes the craftsman approach instead. First identify the core factors that determine success and happiness in your professional and personal life. Then adopt a tool only if its positive impacts on those factors substantially outweigh its negative impacts. A craftsman chooses tools carefully because they serve the work. Newport tells of Forrest Pritchard, a farmer who sold his hay baler after realizing that, once he counted maintenance and the space it took, buying hay was cheaper. Owning a tool is not a reason to keep it. Many successful writers, such as Malcolm Gladwell and Michael Lewis, avoid social media because its benefit to their actual work is small.

To apply this, Newport uses the law of the vital few, the idea that a small number of causes produce most of the results. List the main goals in your professional and personal life, and under each, the two or three most important activities that support it. Then evaluate each tool against those activities. If a tool barely helps the activities that matter most and steals time from them, it fails the test, even if it offers some minor perk.

For social media specifically, Newport suggests a thirty-day break. Quit the services for a month, without deactivating your accounts and without announcing it online. At the end, ask two questions. Would the past thirty days have been notably better if I had been able to use this service? And did people notice I was gone? Newport frames this as finding out whether anyone cared. If the answer to both is no, quit permanently. If the answer is clearly yes, go back. If it is unclear, use your judgment, leaning toward quitting. Most people discover that nobody really noticed, which reveals how little the constant posting mattered, and that their life got worse in no meaningful way.

Rule three ends with a broader point: do not use the internet to entertain yourself. Newport draws on the writer Arnold Bennett, who argued that leisure time deserves as much thought as work. If you spend evenings on endlessly engaging websites, your mind never rests and never practices focus. Instead, plan your free time with demanding, structured activities like reading, hobbies and time with people, so your leisure strengthens your concentration rather than eroding it.

## Rule 4 · Drain the shallows

Rule four is to drain the shallows. Newport begins with the software company 37signals, which experimented with four-day work weeks and reported that the important work still got done, because the shorter week pushed people to drop low-value tasks. The lesson is not that shallow work can disappear entirely, but that it takes up far more time than it deserves. Since people can only sustain a few hours of real depth a day, the aim is to make sure shallow work never crowds out those hours.

The first strategy is to schedule every minute of your workday. At the start of the day, divide your hours into blocks of at least half an hour and assign each block a task, grouping small chores into blocks of their own. When interruptions or bad estimates throw off the plan, simply revise the schedule for the rest of the day. You can add overflow blocks that are used for one task if it runs long and for another if it does not. The point is not rigid obedience, but thoughtfulness. When every hour has a purpose, shallow work is contained in specific blocks rather than seeping into every gap, and you see clearly how your time is actually spent.

The second strategy is to quantify the depth of every activity. Newport offers a test: ask how many months it would take to train a smart recent graduate, with no special training in your field, to do it. If the answer is a short time, the task is shallow and could be done by almost anyone. If it would take many months or years of training to do it well, it is deep and uses your rare skills. This test helps you see which activities deserve your best hours and which you should minimize.

The third strategy is to ask your boss for a shallow work budget. Have an explicit conversation about what fraction of your time should go to shallow work. Newport suggests many knowledge workers would land somewhere between a third and a half. Once agreed, you have permission to say no to extra shallow obligations, and if your job turns out to be mostly shallow, at least you know that clearly.

The fourth strategy is to finish your work by a fixed time each day, which Newport calls fixed-schedule productivity. He commits to ending his workday by about 5:30 in the evening and works backward to fit everything into that window. He describes a Harvard computer scientist who earned tenure while keeping strict limits on her commitments. A fixed end time creates scarcity, and scarcity forces you to decline low-value commitments, think carefully about your schedule, and protect your deep work.

The final strategy is to become hard to reach. Start by filtering email: make people who send you messages do more work, for example by stating on your contact page what kinds of messages you will respond to, so expectations are clear. When you send or reply to email, do more work up front. Instead of a vague reply that invites another round of messages, think about the project the email concerns and propose a full process that brings it to closure, such as offering specific meeting times and saying what happens next. Messages that close loops rather than open them save everyone time. And finally, do not respond to some emails at all. Newport suggests skipping a reply if a message is ambiguous, does not interest you, and nothing bad would happen if you ignored it. Accepting some small inconveniences is the price of protecting your time for the work that matters.

## A deep life

Newport's final argument, from the third chapter of part one and the book's conclusion, is that a deep life is not just more productive but also more meaningful. He makes the case from three angles: the neurological, the psychological and the philosophical.

The neurological argument comes from the science writer Winifred Gallagher. After a cancer diagnosis, she chose to focus her attention on the good things in her life rather than her illness, and she found her experience of that difficult period was better than she expected. Researching the science of attention, she concluded that your world is largely the outcome of what you pay attention to. Newport applies this to work. If you spend your days in deep work, you fill your mind with important, absorbing things. If you spend them in shallow work, your experience of the day is shaped by stress, urgency and trivia. Deep work is not only more productive; it builds a richer inner life.

The psychological argument comes from Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi, the psychologist who studied flow. Using a method in which people were prompted at random times to record what they were doing and how they felt, he found that people were often happier at work than during leisure, because work offered clear goals and challenges. The best moments, he argued, usually occur when the body or mind is stretched to its limits in a voluntary effort to accomplish something difficult. That is exactly what deep work provides, which is why it can feel more satisfying than passive relaxation.

The philosophical argument comes from the philosophers Hubert Dreyfus and Sean Dorrance Kelly. In a world that no longer offers one shared source of meaning, they suggest that craftsmanship can reveal meaning, because a skilled craftsman discovers the value already present in the materials and the work. Newport describes a blacksmith who spent years mastering an ancient sword-making technique. He argues that knowledge work can be approached the same way. When you bring skill and full attention to writing, programming or analysis, flow and craftsmanship turn your job into something that feels important, while scattered attention leaves it feeling empty and frantic.

Newport closes with Bill Gates, who as a young man spent weeks of intense, nearly sleepless concentration writing software for one of the first personal computers, laying the foundation for Microsoft. The story shows that deep work can change what is possible in a career. But Newport is also clear that a deep life is not for everyone. It means saying no to some distractions people enjoy and accepting that you will sometimes be harder to reach. For those who commit, he argues, a deep life is a good life by any measure: it produces more valuable work and a more satisfying existence.
