## The Habit Loop

Charles Duhigg builds his book on one idea from neuroscience and follows it from a single brain to giant corporations and whole social movements. The idea is the habit loop. He introduces it with the story of a retired man named Eugene Pauly, whose brain was ravaged by a viral infection that destroyed the region that forms new memories. Eugene could not say where the kitchen was in his own house or recall a conversation from a minute earlier. Yet over time he learned to take a walk around his block and find his way home, and to go straight to the jar of nuts in the kitchen when he was hungry, with no idea how he knew. Researchers realized he was learning habits without any conscious memory at all, which showed that habits are stored in a different part of the brain from our deliberate thoughts.

Scientists at MIT watched that process in rats learning to run a simple maze to reach a piece of chocolate. At first the rats sniffed and scratched at every wall, and their brains were busy the whole way. As they learned the route, mental activity dropped, until they raced through almost on autopilot. The brain had converted a sequence of actions into one automatic chunk. It does this all the time because it is always looking for ways to save effort, and an efficient brain frees attention for other things. The cost is that once a behavior is chunked, we stop paying attention to it.

Every chunk follows the same three steps. A cue triggers the brain to shift into automatic mode and tells it which habit to use. Then comes a routine, which can be physical, mental or emotional. Finally the routine produces a reward, which helps the brain decide whether this loop is worth remembering. In the rats, brain activity spiked at the start of the maze and at the end but went quiet in the middle, because the cue and the reward are where the real work happens. Over time cue and reward become so tightly linked that the brain begins to anticipate the reward the moment it sees the cue. Duhigg calls that anticipation a craving, and it is what truly powers a habit.

A neuroscientist showed this with a monkey trained to touch a lever whenever a shape appeared on a screen, earning a drop of juice. At first its brain registered pleasure when the juice arrived. Once the habit formed, the pleasure signal fired as soon as the shape appeared, before any juice. If the juice was then delayed, the monkey grew agitated, because it was craving. Marketers learned to build such cravings. Pepsodent toothpaste became a national habit after its ad man pointed people to an easy cue, the film you can feel on your teeth, but what kept them brushing daily was the cool tingle its ingredients left behind, which people came to expect and miss. Febreze flopped as an odor killer, because people who live with a smell stop noticing it, so there was no cue. It took off only when it was relaunched with more perfume as a pleasant finishing touch at the end of cleaning, a reward that fit an existing routine. Studies of exercisers find the same thing: people stick with it when they learn to crave a specific reward, like the endorphin rush or the feeling of accomplishment. So to build a habit, choose a clear cue and a clearly defined reward, and let the anticipation grow.

Because habits run in the basal ganglia, they need little input from the decision-making parts of the brain. That is why you can drive home from work and arrive with no memory of the trip. One study Duhigg cites found that around forty percent of what people do each day is habit rather than decision. It also explains a hard truth: habits never truly disappear. They stay encoded in the brain, waiting for the right cue, which is why a former smoker can relapse years later at a party. But a habit can be overwritten. If you respond to the same cue with a new routine that delivers a similar reward, the new loop can become just as automatic as the old one.

## The Golden Rule of Change

That leads to what Duhigg calls the golden rule of habit change. You usually cannot get rid of a cue, and you cannot simply will away a craving. What you can do is keep the cue and the reward and swap in a new routine. When the old trigger appears, you do something different that still satisfies the underlying craving. Almost every successful program for changing habits, he argues, uses this principle, whether or not it names it.

The clearest example is Alcoholics Anonymous. Scientists long dismissed it because its twelve steps rest on spiritual ideas rather than clinical research, yet it has helped millions of people. Duhigg's explanation is that AA quietly works on the habit loop. Its steps push members to take a full inventory of their lives and recognize the cues that send them toward a drink, such as stress, loneliness or boredom. Then it asks what drinking actually gives them. Often the alcohol itself is not the main reward; people drink for escape, relaxation, emotional release and company. AA supplies new routines that deliver those same rewards: meetings and conversation, a sponsor to call when the urge hits, a room full of people who understand. Drinking had been providing relief and companionship, and the program provides relief and companionship without the bottle.

The same logic powers a clinical technique called habit reversal training. Duhigg describes a graduate student who bit her nails until they bled. A therapist first had her notice the cue, a tension in her fingertips just before she bit, and mark a card every time she felt it, which made an invisible habit visible. Then she learned a competing response: whenever the tension came, she put her hands in her pockets or gripped something, which gave her the physical stimulation she had been seeking. Within about a month the nail biting was gone. Awareness of the cue plus a substitute routine that delivers the same reward is the golden rule in action, and therapists use versions of it for tics, smoking, gambling and more.

Duhigg then takes the rule onto a football field. Tony Dungy, a football coach who took over one of the worst teams in the league, the Tampa Bay Buccaneers, chose not to install a complicated new playbook. Instead he kept the key cues players already watched for on the field and drilled new responses to them until those responses were automatic. Players stopped deliberating and simply reacted, and in a game decided by fractions of a second, that speed won. The team became a contender. But it kept collapsing in the biggest games. Under extreme pressure, players fell back on their old habits, because deep down they did not trust the new system.

That pointed to the missing ingredient: belief. Researchers studying AA found something similar. Many members stayed sober for long stretches using new routines, then relapsed when a crisis hit. Those who held on through the stress were most often the ones who had come to believe in something larger, a higher power in AA's language. What mattered was less religion than belief itself, the conviction that things can get better and that the new routine will hold. Dungy's later team, the Indianapolis Colts, found that belief when a family tragedy of his drew the players together, and they went on to win the Super Bowl. Belief, Duhigg argues, is easiest to build inside a group, because when you watch others change, change starts to seem possible for you. So if you want a new routine to survive real stress, surround yourself with people attempting the same change, or at least people who expect you to succeed.

## Keystone Habits and Small Wins

The second part of the book moves from individuals to organizations, beginning with the idea that some habits matter more than others. These are keystone habits: small changes that start chain reactions, shifting other patterns without anyone targeting them directly. The book opens with one. A woman named Lisa Allen, who had smoked, drunk heavily and struggled with weight and debt, decided on a trip to Egypt to quit smoking so she could trek across the desert. That one change reorganized her life. She started running, changed how she ate, went back to school and bought a home. When scientists scanned her brain, the old patterns were still there, but new ones were overriding them.

When Paul O'Neill became chief executive of Alcoa, a huge and sluggish aluminum company, he told investors at his first meeting that he intended to make it the safest company in America. His priority would be worker safety. He barely mentioned profits, and the investors panicked; one told his clients to sell the stock immediately. But O'Neill chose safety on purpose. It was a goal that unions and executives could not argue with, and achieving it required changing the whole company, since you cannot understand why people get hurt without understanding where the manufacturing process breaks down. He ordered that any injury be reported to him within twenty-four hours, along with a plan to stop it happening again. To meet that rule, managers had to stay in constant touch with the factory floor, so problems and ideas traveled fast. Fixing safety meant fixing communication, fixing equipment and processes, and promoting the people who embraced the new habits. Workers began sending up suggestions that improved efficiency as well as safety. Within a year profits hit a record, and they kept climbing until he retired. The profits followed because safety had changed everything else.

Duhigg identifies three ways keystone habits work. They create small wins, they build structures that help other habits grow, and they create a culture in which new values feel normal. The same is true in personal life. Exercise is a keystone habit for many people. Research shows that when people start exercising regularly, even once a week, they also tend to eat better, smoke less, become more productive and patient, and feel less stressed; their eating, spending, and sleeping tend to improve without direct effort. Families who regularly eat dinner together raise children with better grades and more emotional control. In one weight loss study, people asked to write down everything they ate on at least one day a week soon began doing it more often, spotted patterns in their eating, started planning meals, and lost about twice as much weight as the others. One small structure reorganized everything around it.

The engine behind all of this is small wins. A small win is a modest, visible success that makes the next change seem possible. Early gay rights activists, rather than trying to win everything at once, persuaded the Library of Congress to stop classifying books about homosexuality as sexual abnormality, a small victory that built momentum for bigger ones. Michael Phelps's coach gave him a fixed set of pre-race routines, including stretches, a warm-up, and a nightly visualization of the perfect race that they called watching the videotape. Each step was a small win, so by the time the race began he was riding momentum. When his goggles filled with water during an Olympic final in Beijing, he swam blind, counting strokes as he had rehearsed, and won gold. The lesson is practical: to change a life or a company, do not attack everything at once. Find the one habit that will cascade, and pursue small wins.

## Willpower as a Habit

Duhigg calls willpower the single most important keystone habit for individual success. Studies find that self-discipline predicts students' achievement better than intelligence does, and children who managed to wait for a second marshmallow in a famous experiment tended to do better in school years later. The encouraging news is that willpower is not a fixed trait. It can be strengthened. In one study, people put on a two-month exercise program also began smoking less, drinking less, eating less junk food and studying more. Training self-control in one area built it everywhere.

But willpower also behaves like a muscle in a second way: it tires. In a well-known experiment, hungry students sat in a room with freshly baked cookies. Some were allowed to eat the cookies; others were told to eat only radishes. Then everyone was given a puzzle that could not be solved. The radish eaters gave up much sooner, because they had spent their self-control resisting the cookies. Willpower gets depleted with use, which is why after a long day of holding it together at work you might snap at your family or skip the gym. The solution is to turn self-control into a habit, so it costs less in the moment.

Starbucks built its employee training around this insight. Many of its young workers, Duhigg notes, arrived with little self-discipline, and the company found that teaching willpower habits helped them handle the stress of the job. The method rests on research like a study of elderly patients recovering from hip and knee surgery in Scotland. Some were asked to plan in advance, in writing, exactly what they would do at the moments they knew would be painful, such as the first steps out of a chair or a walk to meet someone at the bus stop. Those who wrote plans recovered far faster, because the hardest moments, the inflection points where people give up, already had a response waiting.

Starbucks taught employees to identify their own inflection points, such as a long line or a rude customer shouting about an order, and to rehearse a set routine for them. One is the LATTE method: listen to the customer, acknowledge the complaint, take action to fix it, thank them, and explain why the problem happened. Employees practiced it in role play until the response was already scripted. When stress hit, they did not need to summon fresh willpower; the habit carried them.

There was one more ingredient. In another experiment, students told to resist cookies were then given a dull task requiring focus. Those who had been asked politely and given a reason held out far better than those who had simply been ordered around. Being commanded drains self-control, while a sense of autonomy restores it. Starbucks responded by giving employees more say over things like how they arranged their workstations and served customers. When people feel in control, their willpower lasts longer. To use this yourself, find the predictable moments where you tend to slip, write down exactly what you will do when they arrive, practice it, and wherever possible give yourself and others real choices.

## The Habits Nobody Designed

Every organization has habits, whether or not anyone planned them. Drawing on the economists Richard Nelson and Sidney Winter, Duhigg explains that much of what companies do comes from routines rather than deliberate decisions. These institutional habits form as rival groups compete for power and credit and gradually settle into truces, unwritten agreements about who does what that keep the peace and let work get done. Truces are useful, but when nobody questions them they can become dangerous.

Rhode Island Hospital, a respected teaching hospital, had developed a culture of deference to surgeons. Doctors held the power, and nurses had learned to work around them, warning each other about difficult surgeons rather than confronting them. In one case an elderly man needed emergency brain surgery. A nurse noticed that the consent form did not say which side of his head to operate on and asked the surgeon to check. He brushed her aside and began, and he operated on the wrong side. The patient later died. It was one of a series of preventable errors at the hospital, and in each case the unspoken truce between surgeons and nurses had silenced the people who might have stopped it.

At King's Cross station on the London Underground, a dropped match apparently fell through a gap in a wooden escalator and started a small fire. A passenger reported smoke to a ticket clerk, but the clerk's job was tickets, so he stayed in his booth. Over decades the Underground had developed a rigid division of responsibilities, in which each department guarded its own territory and employees were trained not to step into anyone else's. Fire safety belonged to no one in particular. Sprinklers that might have doused the flames were not switched on, and no one took charge of an evacuation. The fire grew until it exploded into the ticket hall, killing thirty-one people. It was a fire nobody owned, and the habits that made the system run smoothly every day were the ones that made it deadly that night.

Crises, Duhigg argues, are the moments when organizational habits become visible and can be rewritten. After King's Cross, an investigator's report laid bare how the Underground's rules had blocked anyone from acting, and the system was overhauled, with station managers made responsible for passenger safety. Rhode Island Hospital, after its errors drew public scrutiny and penalties, adopted surgical checklists, cameras in operating rooms and a way for any staff member to report safety concerns, and became a model for other hospitals. The political maxim about never letting a serious crisis go to waste captures the point. Good leaders seize crises, and some even prolong a sense of emergency, because the feeling that something must change is what loosens long-standing truces. The practical lesson is to look at the unwritten rules in your own team and ask which risks no one owns, before a disaster answers the question for you.

## Selling a Habit

Companies study habits because habits drive what we buy, and the retailer Target became a striking example. It assigns each shopper an identification number and records everything tied to it, from purchases to coupons used. Its analysts knew that buying habits are hardest to change except during major life events, such as marriage, moving or a new job, and the biggest of these is a new baby. New parents are exhausted, their routines are up for grabs, and they spend heavily. Because birth records are public, competitors bombard parents right after a birth, so Target wanted to reach them earlier, during pregnancy.

By analyzing purchases, a company statistician found that expectant mothers shifted their shopping in telltale ways, buying things like unscented lotion, certain vitamin supplements and large bags of cotton balls. Together these let Target predict a pregnancy, and even estimate a due date, before many families had told anyone. Duhigg recounts the story of an angry father who stormed into a store demanding to know why his teenage daughter was receiving coupons for cribs and baby clothes. The manager apologized, but when he called back a few days later, the father apologized instead: his daughter was pregnant.

The prediction worked, but customers who noticed it felt watched, and creeped-out customers stop shopping. So Target learned to camouflage what it knew. It mixed the baby coupons in with unrelated offers for things like lawn mowers and wine glasses, so the targeting felt like coincidence. Surrounded by familiar items, the new ones did not set off alarms, and shoppers used them. The lesson Duhigg draws is broad: new habits are adopted most easily when they are dressed in old ones. If you want people to try something unfamiliar, wrap it in something they already know.

The same principle explains how a pop song becomes a hit. When OutKast's song Hey Ya first reached radio, industry experts loved it, but listeners kept changing the station, because it sounded too unfamiliar. Stations solved the problem by sandwiching it between established hits, so listeners heard something comfortable before and after it. With repeated exposure it became familiar, and then it became enormously popular. During the Second World War, the American government faced a similar challenge when it wanted families to eat organ meats so better cuts could go to the troops. Researchers found that success came from making the strange food look familiar, for instance by mixing it into meatloaf and other everyday dishes, rather than lecturing people about nutrition. Whether you are introducing a product, a policy or a personal routine, attach the new behavior to habits people already have, and be aware that marketers are using exactly this principle on you.

## Movements, Free Will, and Change

The final part asks how movements happen. Rosa Parks was not the first Black passenger arrested for refusing to give up a bus seat in Montgomery, so why did her arrest spark the Montgomery bus boycott? Duhigg argues that movements grow in three stages. They begin with strong ties of friendship. Parks was widely respected and unusually well connected, active in civil rights work and her church, with friends across the city's social classes. When she was arrested, those friends acted: a local leader bailed her out, and an activist group printed flyers calling for a one-day boycott. People will take risks for a friend.

A movement grows through weak ties of community, the acquaintances who are not close friends. A sociologist found that most people find new jobs through acquaintances, because weak ties reach more widely. Weak ties also carry peer pressure. In Montgomery, ministers urged their congregations to join, and it became socially costly to stand aside and face neighbors who knew you had not. A study of volunteers for the Freedom Summer voter registration campaign found the same: those who actually went were most often people whose community circles, like church groups, expected them to.

A movement lasts only when it gives people new habits of identity that make it self-propelling. Martin Luther King Jr. taught nonviolence and organized car pools and mass meetings, so participants became people who acted for themselves, and the boycott held for over a year until the Supreme Court struck down bus segregation. Pastor Rick Warren built Saddleback Church the same way, through small groups in members' homes that practice habits of faith without depending on him.

Duhigg closes with a hard question: if habits are automatic, are we responsible for them? He contrasts two cases. A man who strangled his wife during a sleep terror, believing he was fighting an intruder, was found not responsible, because he had no awareness. A woman who lost her family's fortune to compulsive gambling, encouraged by casinos that targeted her, was held responsible. Duhigg sees the difference in awareness: she knew about her habit. Once you know a habit exists, you have the responsibility to change it. Echoing William James, he argues habits are what we choose them to be, and the first step is believing change is possible.

The appendix offers a framework, using his own afternoon cookie habit, which had made him gain weight. First, identify the routine: his was walking to the cafeteria each afternoon, buying a cookie and chatting with colleagues. The cue and reward are harder to see.

Second, experiment with rewards. Some days he walked around the block instead, other days he ate an apple, drank coffee or just chatted with a friend. After each, he wrote down a few words about how he felt and checked fifteen minutes later whether he still wanted the cookie. He learned he was craving socializing, not sugar.

Third, isolate the cue. Almost every cue is a location, a time, an emotional state, other people or an immediately preceding action, so when the urge hits, note all five, and the pattern emerges. His was time: the urge always struck in mid-afternoon. Finally, write a plan that links cue to new routine, such as at three thirty I will walk to a friend's desk and talk for ten minutes. With repetition, the new routine became automatic and the cookie habit faded, which shows that any habit can be changed once you understand it.
